# Orchestrated Plan Execution

When drafting a plan in Plan Mode, consider whether the implementation phase would be more efficient on a cheaper model than the one running the planning conversation. The `cursor-orchestrator` skill exists for exactly this case: plan on a frontier model, execute on Composer 2 (or another tier) via sub-agents.

## When to suggest cursor-orchestrator

Mention the skill proactively when:

- The user is on an expensive model (Opus, GPT-5.4, GPT-5.3 Codex) and the implementation looks like mechanical grunt work (scaffolding, translating a clear spec into files, repetitive edits).
- The plan has many independent todos that could fan out in parallel to several cheaper sub-agents.
- The user mentions cost, budget, or wanting to "delegate" the build.

## What to do

If appropriate, say something like: "This plan could run on a cheaper implementation model via the `cursor-orchestrator` skill — the orchestrator runs on your current model, but each implementation step gets dispatched to Composer 2 (or another tier). Want me to add an `execution:` block so you can `/orchestrate` it?"

Then read `skills/cursor-orchestrator/SKILL.md` (or wherever the skill is installed) for the full flow.

## When NOT to suggest it

- Small plans (one or two steps).
- Plans that are genuinely exploratory — where the implementer needs the same reasoning power as the planner.
- When the user has already declined orchestration in this conversation.
